REFERENCE (Python):
if plugged == "yes":
    if pct == 100: print("Fully charged")
    else:          print("Charging")
else:
    if pct <= 5: print("Plug in now")
    else:
        if pct <= 20: print("Low battery")
        else:         print("Battery fine")

IN JAVASCRIPT: same shape with braces, and compare the text with === . Convert
the percentage with Number() — "100" == 100 happens to be true with == , which
makes a broken program look correct until the day it is not.

TESTING: which condition wins when two are true at once. A phone on 3% that is
charging matches BOTH "plugged in" and "5 or below", and only one answer is
right. Whichever you test first is the one that wins.

MARK WRONG IF:
- 3 yes prints "Plug in now". They checked the battery level before checking
  whether it was plugged in. THIS IS THE TRAP — test 3 yes first.
- 100 no prints "Fully charged". Not plugged in means not charging.
- 5 no prints "Low battery" — the boundary; 5 or below is "Plug in now".
- 21 no prints "Low battery" — the other boundary.

CHECK: 3 yes, then 100 no, then 5 no.

REFERENCE (Python):
if a1 == a2:
    if b1 == b2:
        if a1 > b1:   print("Player 1")
        else:
            if b1 > a1: print("Player 2")
            else:       print("Draw")
    else:
        print("Player 1")
else:
    if b1 == b2:
        print("Player 2")
    else:
        if a1 + a2 > b1 + b2:   print("Player 1")
        else:
            if b1 + b2 > a1 + a2: print("Player 2")
            else:                 print("Draw")

IN JAVASCRIPT: same shape with braces. Convert all four with Number() — a1 + a2
on strings concatenates, so "6" + "5" is "65" and every total is nonsense.

TESTING: a rule that overrides the normal comparison entirely. The totals are a
red herring until you know whether either player rolled a double, so the question
is which test comes first — and there are four combinations of doubles to work
through, not two.

MARK WRONG IF:
- 2 2 6 5 prints "Player 2". They compared totals first, so 11 beat 4. THIS IS
  THE TRAP and it is the first example for that reason.
- 3 3 5 5 prints "Player 1" or "Draw". Both rolled doubles, so the higher double
  wins — they either stopped at "Player 1 has a double" or compared totals.
- 4 4 4 4 prints "Player 1". Identical doubles is a draw.
- 6 3 5 4 prints anything but "Draw". Equal totals, no doubles.

CHECK: 2 2 6 5, then 3 3 5 5, then 4 4 4 4, then 6 3 5 4.
